<p>
  The Bible is not only a book to be read. It is a book to be <em>studied</em>, and there is a difference. Reading gives you familiarity. Study gives you understanding. And understanding is what changes a life.
</p>

<p>
  Digging into God&rsquo;s Word is the privilege and the duty of every believer, not only pastors and scholars. The Holy Spirit is the ultimate teacher of Scripture, and He
  abides in every person who belongs to Christ (1 John 2:27). You do not need a seminary degree.
  You need a believing heart, a willingness to work, and a few sound principles to guide you.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Before You Open the Book: Prerequisites for Understanding</h2>

<p>
  The most important prerequisite for Bible study is not intelligence. It is a regenerate heart. The natural person does not accept the things of the Spirit of God, for they are folly
  to him (1 Corinthians 2:14). But to those who have been born again, the Spirit illumines the
  Word. He opens eyes that were once blind to its truth.
</p>

<p>
  Come to Scripture in prayer. Ask God to teach you before you read a single verse. Psalm 119:18
  is a perfect model: <em>&ldquo;Open my eyes, that I may behold wondrous things out of your
  law.&rdquo;</em> For a growing Christian, diligent study is not optional. It is the road to maturity itself (2 Timothy 2:15).
</p>

