<p>
  As you read, write down the theme of each chapter on a small card. Memorize those themes as
  you go. You will be amazed at how quickly you begin to know where things are in the Bible
  and what each book is about.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Step 2: Interpret</h2>

<p>
  As you read, always keep one question in mind: <strong>&ldquo;What does this mean?&rdquo;</strong>
  Philip asked the Ethiopian eunuch exactly this in Acts 8:30: <em>&ldquo;Do you understand what
  you are reading?&rdquo;</em> It is not enough to read the text and jump straight to application.
  You must first determine what it <em>means</em>, or the application may be wrong.
</p>

<p>
  The most foundational principle of interpretation is this: <strong>interpret the Bible with
  the Bible.</strong> The Reformers called this the <em>analogy of faith</em>: Holy Scripture is its own interpreter. The supreme standard for understanding any verse is
  the overall teaching of Scripture. If your interpretation of one passage contradicts the
  clear teaching of another, your interpretation needs adjustment, not the Bible.
</p>

<p>
  Five rules keep you on track. Think of them as the tick marks on the rod:
</p>


