<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Read it like any other book.</strong> God communicated through real human authors
    using real words, grammar, and literary forms. If it is poetry, read it as poetry. If it
    is history, read it as history. Do not impose a mystical meaning that bypasses the plain
    sense of the text.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Take the literal sense seriously.</strong> Interpret the text according to its
    natural, intended meaning in its original context. That does not mean ignoring figures of speech. It means not reading into the text something that is not there.
    The literal sense is the author&rsquo;s intended sense.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Let the explicit govern the implicit.</strong> Do not build major doctrines on
    what is only implied. Let the clear, explicit teaching of Scripture anchor your
    understanding of what is harder to interpret.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Let the clear interpret the obscure.</strong> Every Bible reader encounters
    difficult passages. The rule is simple: let the clear passages illuminate the obscure
    ones, never the other way around. Never overturn plain teaching based on an
    ambiguous text.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Use cross-references and concordances.</strong> Let Scripture shed light on
    Scripture. Free tools like
    <a href="https://www.blueletterbible.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Blue Letter Bible</a>
    give you access to original words, cross-references, and commentaries at no cost.
  </li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h2>Step 3: Evaluate</h2>

<p>
  After reading and forming an interpretation, consult others to confirm you are on solid
  ground. Read Bible introductions, commentaries, and background books. The church has been
  studying Scripture for two thousand years. Stand on the shoulders of godly men who came
  before you. Reading commentaries by men like John Calvin and Matthew Henry is an excellent
  way to benefit from scholars whose work still serves the church centuries later.
</p>

<p>
  If your interpretation puts a passage in conflict with the rest of Scripture, the interpretation needs adjusting, not the Bible. And if a passage remains unclear
  after your own study, bring it to your pastor or a trusted teacher. That is not weakness.
  That is wisdom.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Step 4: Apply</h2>

<p>
  Bible study that never changes how you live has missed the point. After reading, interpreting,
  and evaluating, ask the most personal question of all:
  <strong>&ldquo;What does this mean for <em>me</em>?&rdquo;</strong>
</p>

<p>
  James 1:22 is direct: <em>&ldquo;Be doers of the word, and not hearers only, deceiving
  yourselves.&rdquo;</em> Sound interpretation must lead to obedient application. The goal of Bible study is not information. It is transformation.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Step 5: Correlate</h2>

<p>
  The final step is connecting what you have learned in a particular passage to the larger
  teaching of the whole Bible. Scripture is one book in sixty-six parts. Its truths are
  taught repeatedly, in a variety of ways and circumstances, from Genesis to Revelation.
  By cross-referencing and correlating what you study, you begin to build a sound doctrinal
  foundation on which to stand for the rest of your life.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Read with Humility</h2>

<p>
  Running through all of this must be a spirit of humility. It is possible to be sincere in your
  understanding of a passage and sincerely wrong. Humility keeps us open to correction: by the text itself, by the church, by godly teachers, and by the Holy Spirit. Pride in Bible
  study is especially dangerous because it can dress itself up as conviction.
</p>

<p>
  The great challenge of biblical interpretation is not technical. It is spiritual.
  The person who approaches God&rsquo;s Word with a humble, teachable heart, willing to be
  changed by what it says, is already well ahead of the person who comes to the text looking
  for confirmation of what they already believe.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Practical Tips to Get Started Today</h2>

<div class="kit">
  <a href="https://www.crossway.org/bibles/esv-study-bible-case/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg><use href="/assets/icons.svg#icon-bible"/></svg>A good study Bible</a>
  <span><svg><use href="/assets/icons.svg#icon-letter"/></svg>Start with 1 John</span>
  <span><svg><use href="/assets/icons.svg#icon-notebook"/></svg>Keep a notebook</span>
  <a href="https://www.blueletterbible.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg><use href="/assets/icons.svg#icon-search"/></svg>Free online tools</a>
  <span><svg><use href="/assets/icons.svg#icon-people"/></svg>Study in community</span>
  <span><svg><use href="/assets/icons.svg#icon-sun"/></svg>Read it every day</span>
  <span><svg><use href="/assets/icons.svg#icon-speak"/></svg>Teach what you learn</span>
</div>

<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Get a good study Bible.</strong> The
    <a href="https://www.crossway.org/bibles/esv-study-bible-case/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ESV Study Bible</a>
    is one of the most thorough and trustworthy study Bibles available, with extensive notes,
    maps, articles, and cross-references to help you understand the text in context.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Start with 1 John.</strong> It is short, rich, and covers the essentials of the
    Christian life. Read it through every day for thirty days and watch what happens.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Keep a notebook.</strong> Write down what you observe, what you do not understand,
    and what you sense God applying to your life. Writing forces clarity of thought.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Use free online tools.</strong>
    <a href="https://www.blueletterbible.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Blue Letter Bible</a>
    gives you access to concordances, original language tools, cross-references, and
    commentaries at no cost.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Study in community.</strong> The teaching ministry of a local church is
    indispensable to personal Bible study. Do not study in isolation. Bring your questions
    to a pastor, a small group, or a trusted fellow believer.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Read it every day.</strong> Joshua 1:8 commands meditating on God&rsquo;s Word
    day and night. Consistency over time is far more powerful than occasional marathon sessions.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Teach what you are learning.</strong> The best way to truly know Scripture is to
    share it with someone else. The things you understand well enough to explain are the things
    that will stay with you for a lifetime.
  </li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h2>A Closing Word</h2>

<p>
  People like to say the Bible can be made to say anything. In the hands of someone careless, or someone with an agenda, that can look true.
  The answer is not to read less. It is to read better, more carefully, and more prayerfully,
  with sound principles guiding every step.
</p>

<p>
  Open God&rsquo;s Word expecting Him to speak. Come to it daily. Come to it humbly. Come to it
  hungry. He has promised that His Word will not return to Him empty (Isaiah 55:11). That promise
  is for you.
</p>

